  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front is expected to cross through \nthe region on Thursday, resulting in widespread showers and \nthunderstorms. Flash flooding and severe weather, including all \nhazards are possible.\n\nTo the south of a closed upper low over eastern Canada, broad \ntroughing will be in place across much of the Northeast and Mid-\nAtlantic. An embedded mid-level shortwave will pass through the\nbase of the trough on Thursday. At the surface, a warm front\nwill move into portions of the area tonight as a cold front \nwill begin to approach the area on Thursday.\n\nWith guidance coming into better agreement with the timing and\nplacement of frontal boundaries in our area, there has been an \nuptick in confidence in what the evolution could be on Thursday.\nFor now, multiple rounds of widespread showers and storms are \nexpected. With the cold front approaching from the west, and a \nwarm front expected to set-up across the southern half of the \narea across the Delmarva and into southern New Jersey, we will \nhave to watch these areas for severe weather Thursday afternoon \nand evening. In fact, SPC has upgraded portions of the area to a\nSLIGHT risk for severe weather (Level 2/5) with a MARGINAL risk\nelsewhere. Bulk shear values up to 30-40 kt support organized \nclusters where locally damaging wind gusts are possible. With \nthe warm front draped over portions of the area, there will be \na localized corridor of a tornado threat, especially where any \nstorm is able to latch onto the boundary. The best guess of \nwhere this may occur is somewhere in the Delmarva to southern \nNew Jersey corridor where SPC has introduced a 2% tornado risk \nwith locally backed flow at the surface. Cannot rule out\nisolated large hail as well as temperatures aloft are rather\ncool for this time of year. There should be an abundance of\nclouds in place on Thursday, so this could hinder the magnitude\nof the severe threat to some degree.\n\nThe other, and possibly more concerning threat may be the risk \nand potential for flash flooding across portions of the area. \nMost guidance continues to depict a very moisture rich airmass \nsurging northward on Thursday where PWAT values are expected to \ntop out around 2.2-2.6 inches. Model forecast soundings also \nshow tall, skinny CAPE profiles and a relatively deep warm cloud\nlayer. The one saving grace is that the overall shear vectors \nindicate that storms should be 'relatively' quick moving.\nHowever, any given storm will be capable of producing highly \nefficient rain rates, at times around 2 to 3+ inches per hour. \nWith this being said, WPC has maintained a SLIGHT risk (Level \n2/4) for excessive rainfall on Thursday, but has expanded the \nrisk further north to incorporate northern New Jersey. In \ngeneral, expecting anywhere between 1 to 2 inches of rain to \nfall, with localized higher amounts up to 3 to 5 inches. This is\nsupported by most of the 12Z guidance suite today, especially\nwhere the HREF LPMM show localized bullseyes of higher rainfall.\nGiven the increased confidence in flooding potential, have \nopted to issue a Flash Flood Watch for portions of southeast PA,\nnorthern Delaware, and New Jersey from 12 PM Thursday through 2\nAM Friday. \n\nThe severe and flooding rain threat should diminish overnight into\nFriday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Conditions look to remain unsettled through the\nweekend, with daily chances of showers and storms each day.\n\nMid to long range NWP and AI guidance suggests that the pattern\ncould become unsettled Friday night and into the weekend. \nDepending on how the frontal boundary evolution pans out on \nThursday, will impact the weather over the weekend. Most \nguidance does suggest that the frontal boundary will stall over \nor in close proximity of the area where several areas of low \npressure may ride along the stalled boundary through the \nweekend. PoPs are currently in the 40-80% range on Saturday and\n30-60% range on Sunday. Given the present uncertainties in \ntiming and placement of the frontal features and progression in\nthe long wave pattern, have opted to keep very close to the NBM.\n\nIn terms of temps, highs and lows will both be quite seasonable\nfor late August, but humidity levels may be uncomfortable at \ntimes.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Fair weather is expected to return for early\nnext week.\n\nHigh pressure looks to bring fair and tranquil weather to start\nnext week. The pattern potentially becomes more active again\ntowards the middle to end of next week.",
